cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  CARVE-V: Core Area Reduction by Verified Elimination — a workload-driven ISA
  subsetting flow for CVA6
message: >-
  If you use this specification, the isaprof instrument, or results derived from
  them, please cite this repository.
type: software
abstract: >-
  A project specification and measurement instrument for deriving an instruction
  subset from a target workload, pruning an application-class RISC-V core
  accordingly, proving the pruned core equivalent to the baseline on the
  surviving subset, and preserving binary compatibility through M-mode
  trap-and-emulate handlers. The reclaimed area is reinvested in a CV-X-IF
  attached TinyML datapath. The target application is an always-on micro-UAV
  perception node using mmWave radar and IMU.
authors:
  - name: The CARVE-V contributors
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.1.0
keywords:
  - RISC-V
  - CVA6
  - ISA subsetting
  - instruction set customisation
  - trap-and-emulate
  - formal equivalence
  - CV-X-IF
  - TinyML
  - edge AI
  - ASIC tapeout
references:
  - type: article
    title: Flexing RISC-V Instruction Subset Processors to Extreme Edge
    url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.04567
    notes: >-
      Closest prior work on instruction subsetting. Targets tiny cores; does not
      preserve binary compatibility and does not formally verify the pruning.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      NeCTAr: A Heterogeneous RISC-V SoC for Language Model Inference in Intel 16
    url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14708
    notes: >-
      Feasibility precedent for a university test chip. Not prior art on ISA
      subsetting.
  - type: software
    title: CVA6 — the CORE-V application-class RISC-V core
    url: https://github.com/openhwgroup/cva6
  - type: software
    title: Chipyard — an agile RISC-V SoC design framework
    url: https://github.com/ucb-bar/chipyard
